{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":5,"query":"name=Ehrenreich&limit=10&title=Nickel and Dimed","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Nickel and dimed "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Holden, Joan","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"drama"},{"@authority":"Library of Congress Subject Headings","#text":"American drama."},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Drama"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Drama"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Dramatists Play Service"},"dateIssued":"2005"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"67 p. ; 19 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Can a middle-aged, middle-class woman survive, when she suddenly has to make beds all day in a hotel and live on $7 an hour? Maybe. But one $7-an-hour job won't pay the rent: she'll have to do back-to-back shifts, as a chambermaid and a waitress. ... The play shows us the life a third of working Americans now lead, and makes us angry that anyone should have to live it."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by Joan Holden."},"\"Based on Nickel and dimed, on (not) getting by in America, by Barbara Ehrenreich.\" -- T.p."],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Minimum wage","geographic":"United States","genre":"Drama"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Working poor","geographic":"United States","genre":"Drama"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Unskilled labor","geographic":"United States","genre":"Drama"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Poverty","geographic":"United States","genre":"Drama"}],"classification":{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"PS3515.O3318 N53 2005"},"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Nickel and dimed : on (not) getting by in America"},"name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Ehrenreich, Barbara."}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990098806460203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0822220423"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"62744804"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000109426663","#text":"Arthur and Elizabeth Schlesinger Library on the History of Women in America, Radcliffe Institute for Advanced Study, Harvard University"}},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"PS3515.O3318 N53 2005"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":["Schlesinger","Widener"]},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990098806460203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"009880646"}},"processingDate":"2026-02-09T17:27Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"GZD"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"051227"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20060301"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990098806460203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Nickel and dimed ","subTitle":"on (not) getting by in America "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Ehrenreich, Barbara","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Creative nonfiction"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Metropolitan Books"},"dateIssued":"2001","edition":"1st ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"221 p. ; 22 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Millions of Americans work full-time, year-round, for poverty-level wages. In 1998, the author decided to join them. She was inspired in part by the rhetoric surrounding welfare reform, which promised that a job, any job, could be the ticket to a better life. But how does anyone survive, let alone prosper, on six to seven dollars an hour? To find out, she left her home, took the cheapest lodgings she could find, and accepted whatever jobs she was offered as a woefully inexperienced homemaker returning to the workforce. So began a grueling, hair raising, and darkly funny odyssey through the underside of working America. Moving from Florida to Maine to Minnesota, she worked as a waitress, a hotel maid, a cleaning woman, a nursing home aide, and a Wal-Mart sales clerk. Very quickly, she discovered that no job is truly \"unskilled,\" that even the lowliest occupations require exhausting mental and muscular effort. She also learned that one job is not enough; you need at least two if you intend to live indoors."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Getting ready -- Serving in Florida -- Scrubbing in Maine -- Selling in Minnesota -- Evaluatio